(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609111700-the-table-layer-found-a-corrupted-field-name-on-its-first-live-run")
  (:adr/topic "q9-entity-name-substitution")
  (:adr/title "The table layer found a corrupted field name on its first live run, and amu check had passed it")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/com-agora"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609111600-the-entity-table-is-derivable-and-varies-so-it-is-the-layer-worth-adding"))
  (:adr/body
  "## What happened

The entity-table layer was added last iteration on the argument that it derives
from the repository's own spec table on both sides and therefore VARIES, unlike
`page-limit`. Its first run against three fresh repositories produced a
disagreement:

    com-agones           table 6 of 6
    com-agora            table 5 of 6, index 3 differing
    com-airbus-analytx   table 6 of 6

`com-agora` index 3 is `Room`. Reading both renderings:

    component  \"maxChannelicipants:int,recording:bool\"
    oracle     \"maxParticipants:int,recording:bool\"

`maxParticipants` had become `maxChannelicipants`.

## The mechanism

The component generator replaces the template's entity names with the target's.
The template's root entity is `Part`; agora's is `Channel`. The replacement was
an unanchored substring, so it fired inside `maxParticipants` -- a FIELD name
that merely contains the letters of an entity name.

This is the same shape as the `.clj` / `.cljc` prefix aliasing this workspace
hit earlier in the same week: a substring replacement reaching inside a longer
word.

## Why nothing else caught it

`fields-of` and `coerce-table-of` return STRINGS, so:

  - `amu check` returns `:ok true`. The compiler has no schema for their contents.
  - The generator's evidence floor scans for surviving TEMPLATE stems
    (`abbrobot`, `abb_robotics`). A corrupted TARGET word is not a template
    stem, so it is invisible to that check by construction.
  - The four other harness layers pass: route count, as-int, as-bool and
    page-limit never read a field name.

The component shipped, compiled, ran, and answered questions about a field
called `maxChannelicipants`.

## Blast radius, and a correction to my own first measurement

Every pilot's `fields-of` and `coerce-table-of` were compared against its own
`entity-specs`. The answer is ONE repository, two rows.

The first version of that scan said three repositories. Its field-name regex was
`[A-Za-z]+`, which dropped the digits from `last4` and `countryIso3` and
declared `com-ach-network` and `com-aftership` mismatched when their components
are correct. The scanner was wrong, not the components -- worth recording,
because a corruption scan that over-reports sends someone to regenerate working
code.

## The fix and its control

Entity names are now replaced on a word boundary. Prefixes and namespace tokens
keep the plain form: `abbrobot_par` cannot occur inside another identifier, and
anchoring those would break `src/abb_robotics/`.

Controls, both required:

  **it repairs the break** -- com-agora regenerated, `amu check :ok true`,
  compiled, executed, table 6 of 6 where it was 5 of 6.

  **it changes nothing that was right** -- com-accela, where the substitution
  also fires (`Part` -> `Project`) and the output was already correct,
  regenerates byte-identical.

A re-scan of all twenty now reports zero mismatched rows.

## What this says about the layer

The table layer was added on a theoretical argument -- derivable, varies,
catches a defect class the evidence floor cannot see. It found a real instance
on its first live run, in a repository nobody suspected, in a component that had
passed every check it was subject to.

The four layers that preceded it have never disagreed except on the one row they
are designed to disagree on. That is not a criticism of them; it is the reason
this one was worth adding, and the reason the fixture-shaped layers are still
worth wanting.")))
